{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims reinstatement is preserved because the meet is strictly more skeptical than any single school, but the meet-of-schools construction violates the reinstatement-as-derived-property guarantee. In Dung-style semantics, reinstatement requires that if an argument A is defeated and B (which defeats A's sole attacker) is accepted, then A is reinstated (accepted). Under pointwise meet, B may be accepted in the global meet while A is rejected because A is not accepted by at least one school — even though that school accepts B. For a concrete counterexample: consider two arguments A and B with a single attacker C, where B attacks C. School 1's grounded extension is {B, A} (B defeats C, reinstating A), and School 2's grounded extension is {B} (accepts B but rejects A due to a policy that is more skeptical about reinstatement). The meet is {B}: A is rejected globally despite its attacker C being defeated by an accepted argument B. Reinstatement is not derived — it is violated. This is a direct failure of the reinstatement guarantee the problem requires.","counterexample":["Arguments: A, B, C","Attack relation: C attacks A; B attacks C","School 1 grounded extension: {B, A} — B defeats C, so A is reinstated","School 2 grounded extension: {B} — B is accepted but A is not reinstated under School 2's policy","Meet (pointwise intersection): {B}","Result: A is rejected in the meet even though its only attacker C is defeated by accepted B — reinstatement fails"],"target":"010d99f82b442c92e3e7b63f664ae8c1c02d4bbd7fabb66c3248abfa1019da05"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"9b08893fbb1d1a4b718a4536e048aa8e0315e5ae7ead814d753cd0cbce9ce3c6"}]}